ON-DEMAND
"## ON-DEMAND
With this model, I pay for the AWS resources based on how much I use them.
I don't need to make a long-term commitment. This is useful when I am
learning, testing, or when my usage is not predictable.".

RESERVED
"## RESERVED INSTANCES

With Reserved Instances, I make a commitment to use an eligible EC2
configuration for a longer period. In return, I can get a discount
compared with On-Demand pricing. This is useful when I know that I will
need the resources for a long time.".

SPOT
"## SPOT

Spot Instances allow me to use unused AWS computing capacity at a much
lower price. However, AWS can interrupt the instance when it needs the
capacity back. Therefore, Spot Instances are useful for workloads that
can tolerate interruptions.". 


## MY UNDERSTANDING

As a learner, I think On-Demand is useful because I can use AWS resources
when I need them without making a long-term commitment. Spot Instances
can also be useful for suitable practice workloads because they are much
cheaper, but I need to accept that they can be interrupted.
